# Summary of Title VIII (Committee on Health, Education, Labor, and Pensions)

**Sec. 80001. Exemption of certain assets.**
Amends the Higher Education Act's need-analysis formula (Section 480(f)(2)) so that the net value of a family farm on which the family resides, a family-owned small business with 100 or fewer full-time (or full-time equivalent) employees, and a family-owned commercial fishing business (including vessels and permits) are excluded from assets when calculating federal student aid eligibility. The change takes effect July 1, 2026, and applies to award year 2026–2027 and each subsequent award year.

**Sec. 81001. Loan limits for graduate/professional students and parent borrowers; termination of Grad PLUS loans.**
Terminates Federal Direct PLUS loans for graduate and professional students for periods of instruction beginning on or after July 1, 2026, and sets new annual Unsubsidized Stafford limits of $20,500 for graduate students and $50,000 for professional students, with aggregate limits of $100,000 (graduate-only), $200,000 (professional), or $200,000 minus prior graduate borrowing (combined). Parent PLUS loans are capped at $20,000 annually and $65,000 in aggregate per dependent student, and a lifetime aggregate cap of $257,500 applies to all student borrowing (excluding parent PLUS loans), without regard to amounts repaid or forgiven. Less-than-full-time students face proportionally reduced limits, and institutions may impose lower program-level caps applied consistently. Current students who, as of June 30, 2026, are enrolled and have borrowed may continue under prior limits for the lesser of three academic years or their remaining program length.

**Sec. 82001. Loan repayment.**
Repeals income-contingent repayment (Section 455(e)) and requires all borrowers with income-contingent loans to select a new plan before July 1, 2028, defaulting them into the Repayment Assistance Plan (RAP) or income-based repayment (IBR) if they fail to choose. For loans made on or after July 1, 2026, borrowers may choose only between a standard plan (10/15/20/25-year terms based on principal tiers of <$25,000, $25,000–$50,000, $50,000–$100,000, and ≥$100,000) or RAP, under which monthly payments are calculated from a base payment of $120 at $10,000 AGI rising to 10% of AGI above $100,000, minus $50 per dependent, with a $10 minimum. RAP forgives remaining balances after 360 qualifying monthly payments, subsidizes unpaid interest, and makes $50 matching principal payments when a payment reduces principal by less than $50. Parent PLUS loans and consolidation loans that paid off such loans ("excepted loans") are excluded from RAP and IBR and must be repaid under the standard plan; IBR is tightened to 15% of discretionary income above 150% of the poverty line, with automatic income recertification via IRS data.

**Sec. 82002. Deferment; forbearance.**
Sunsets unemployment and economic hardship deferments for borrowers receiving loans on or after July 1, 2027. Such borrowers may receive only one forbearance under Section 428(c)(3)(B) not exceeding 9 months during any 24-month period.

**Sec. 82003. Loan rehabilitation.**
Allows borrowers to rehabilitate a defaulted FFEL or Direct loan two times (instead of one) and a Perkins loan twice (instead of once), effective July 1, 2027. For loans made under part D on or after July 1, 2027, the total minimum monthly rehabilitation payment is set at $10.

**Sec. 82004. Public service loan forgiveness.**
Amends PSLF (Section 455(m)) to count on-time payments made under the new Repayment Assistance Plan toward the 120-payment forgiveness requirement, alongside payments under the standard plan and the pre-repeal income-contingent and income-based plans.

**Sec. 82005. Student loan servicing.**
Provides the Secretary of Education an additional $1,000,000,000 in mandatory funds (available until expended) for administrative costs under the Direct Loan and FFEL programs, including servicing of the direct student loan programs.

**Sec. 83001. Pell Grant eligibility.**
Requires foreign income (as described in Section 480(b)(5)) of students and parents to be added to adjusted gross income for Pell eligibility determinations for academic years beginning on or after July 1, 2026, and sunsets the prior simplified adjusted-gross-income look-through under Section 479A(b)(1)(B)(v). Beginning July 1, 2026, students whose Student Aid Index equals or exceeds twice the maximum Pell Grant for the award year are ineligible for a Pell Grant.

**Sec. 83002. Workforce Pell Grants.**
Creates a Workforce Pell Grant program starting with the July 1, 2026 award year for students in short-term "eligible workforce programs" of at least 150 but fewer than 600 clock hours (or equivalent credit hours) over at least 8 but fewer than 15 weeks, excluding correspondence courses and graduate credentials. Programs must be approved by the State Governor as aligned with high-skill, high-wage, or in-demand sectors, lead to stackable/portable credentials or a single recognized occupational credential, prepare students for further certificate or degree programs with accepted credit, and be certified by the Secretary to have operated for at least 1 year with at least 70% completion rates (within 150% of normal time), at least 70% job placement within 180 days, and tuition and fees not exceeding the "value-added earnings" of completers (median earnings minus 150% of the poverty line, adjusted for regional price parities). Awards follow Pell terms, are prorated for short programs, cannot be received concurrently with regular Pell Grants, and count toward the lifetime Pell duration limit.

**Sec. 83003. Pell shortfall.**
Raises the Pell Grant funding trigger in Section 401(b)(7)(A)(iii) from $2,170,000,000 to $12,670,000,000, increasing the appropriation ceiling used to cover shortfalls in the Pell program.

**Sec. 83004. Federal Pell Grant exclusion relating to other grant aid.**
Beginning July 1, 2026, a student is ineligible for a Federal Pell Grant for any period in which the student receives non-federal grant aid—from States, institutions, or private sources—equal to or exceeding the student's cost of attendance for that period.

**Sec. 84001. Ineligibility based on low earning outcomes.**
Beginning July 1, 2026, institutions become ineligible to use Title IV funds for any undergraduate, graduate, professional, or graduate certificate program whose completers' median earnings fall below the median earnings of a comparable working adult (aged 25–34, not enrolled, with only a high school diploma for undergraduate programs or only a bachelor's degree for graduate programs) in at least 2 of the 3 preceding years, using Census data benchmarked to the institution's State (or nationally if fewer than 50% of students reside in-State, with field-of-study comparisons for graduate programs). Cohorts smaller than 30 individuals must be aggregated across years and equivalent-length programs to reach 30. Programs failing for one year must notify enrolled students of the risk, institutions may appeal through a Secretary-established process, and ineligible programs may apply to regain eligibility after at least 2 years.

**Sec. 85001. Delay of rule relating to borrower defense to repayment.**
For loans first originating before July 1, 2035, blocks the November 1, 2022 borrower-defense-to-repayment final regulations (87 Fed. Reg. 65904) from taking effect, effective upon enactment. The 2020 borrower defense regulations are restored and revived as they existed on July 1, 2020.

**Sec. 85002. Delay of rule relating to closed school discharges.**
For loans first originating before July 1, 2035, blocks the closed-school discharge provisions (34 C.F.R. §§ 674.33(g), 682.402(d), and 685.214) added or amended by the November 1, 2022 final regulations (87 Fed. Reg. 65904), effective upon enactment. Those regulatory sections apply as if the 2022 amendments had never been made.

**Sec. 86001. Garden of Heroes.**
Appropriates $40,000,000 to the National Endowment for the Humanities for fiscal year 2025, remaining available through fiscal year 2028, to procure statues for the National Garden of American Heroes as described in Executive Orders 13934, 13978, and 14189.

**Sec. 87001. Potential sponsor vetting for unaccompanied alien children appropriation.**
Appropriates $300,000,000 to the Office of Refugee Resettlement for fiscal year 2025, available through September 30, 2028, solely for vetting and placement safety costs. Funds may be used for background checks on potential sponsors and all adults in their households (including names, SSNs/taxpayer IDs, birthdates, validated residence, interviews and suitability studies, sex-offender registry checks, public records checks, and fingerprint-based national criminal history checks), home studies, examinations of children for gang-related tattoos or markings (and covering them while in ORC care), data systems to identify child labor exploitation and trafficking, and coordination with State child welfare agencies on placements. [end of text]


